  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An alkanol carries a hydroxyl group, –OH, on a saturated carbon.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Replacing one hydrogen of an alkane by –OH gives CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The carbon carrying the –OH is attached to only one other carbon, so ethanol is primary.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The carbinol carbon is attached to two other carbons, so propan-2-ol is secondary.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Yeast provides the enzymes — zymase — that convert glucose to ethanol and carbon dioxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The yeast works fastest at about 25–35 °C; too hot denatures its enzymes, too cold slows them down.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    In air, bacteria oxidise the ethanol to ethanoic acid, which is why a fermenting vessel is kept closed.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One mole of glucose gives two moles of ethanol and two of carbon dioxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Above about 14 % the ethanol kills the yeast, so a stronger product needs distillation.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ethanol boils at 78 °C and water at 100 °C, so fractional distillation separates them.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    C₂H₄ + H₂O over H₃PO₄ at about 300 °C and high pressure gives ethanol.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The –OH group allows hydrogen bonding, which needs more energy to break than the van der Waals forces in ethane.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Its –OH group forms hydrogen bonds to water molecules, so no separate layer forms.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2C₂H₅OH + 2Na → 2C₂H₅ONa + H₂; the O–H hydrogen is the one released.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    C₂H₅OH + PCl₅ → C₂H₅Cl + POCl₃ + HCl, and the white fumes of HCl are the visible sign.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The acid removes the elements of water — dehydration — and leaves the double bond of ethene.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The dichromate, orange Cr(VI), is reduced to the green Cr(III) as it oxidises the alcohol.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The first product is the aldehyde, which is then oxidised further to the acid unless it is distilled off.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    There is no hydrogen left on the carbinol carbon after the first oxidation, so the product is the ketone.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The oxidising agent must remove hydrogen from the carbinol carbon, and a tertiary alcohol has none.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Acidified dichromate is the standard oxidising agent; with excess reagent and warming, the acid is the product.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Weakness refers to the extent of ionisation: only about 1 % of the molecules ionise in dilute solution.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Partial ionisation gives a hydrogen ion concentration far below 0.1 mol dm⁻³, so the pH is about 3.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An acid plus a carbonate gives a salt, water and carbon dioxide — the effervescence is the test.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ethanoic acid plus a base gives the salt of the acid — sodium ethanoate, CH₃COONa — and water.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The acid and the alcohol join with the loss of water to give the ester, ethyl ethanoate.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Vinegar is 4–8 % ethanoic acid, produced by the bacterial oxidation of ethanol.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Acetobacter oxidises ethanol to ethanoic acid in the presence of air — the same reaction that sours palm wine.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The acid fizzes with sodium carbonate and does not react with sodium metal vigorously; the alcohol reacts with sodium but gives no gas with carbonate.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n = cV/1000 = 0.200 × 25.0/1000 = 5.00 × 10⁻³ mol.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n = 0.100 × 0.500 = 0.0500 mol, and M = 60, so m = 3.0 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Its –OH end mixes with water and its hydrocarbon end with oils, so it carries an organic active ingredient into solution.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Denaturing adds methanol and a bitter substance, which makes the ethanol undrinkable and untaxed.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O; with enough air the products are carbon dioxide and water only.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ethanol raises the octane number and is produced from crops, so it is used as a blending agent.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is a secondary alcohol, so oxidation removes the hydrogen from the carbinol carbon and leaves the ketone, propanone.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2C₂H₅OH + 2Na → 2C₂H₅ONa + H₂, so the ratio of alcohol to sodium is one to one.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Copper is below hydrogen in the reactivity series, so it cannot displace hydrogen from an acid.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An alkanoic acid is an alkane with one hydrogen replaced by the carboxyl group: CₙH₂ₙ₊₁COOH.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The ester, ethyl ethanoate, is volatile and has the fruity smell associated with esters.
  ]

]

